---
import ServiceSection from "../components/ServiceSection.astro";
import Endpoint from "../components/Endpoint.astro";
import RustSchema from "../components/RustSchema.astro";
---

<ServiceSection id="service-conn">
  <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn</code> — connection control</Fragment>
<p>Connection endpoints report failures and bootstrap state. They activate
    transport protection. They also carry routed application frames.</p>
    <p class="endpoint-index">
      <a href="#conn-boom">Conn/1</a><a href="#conn-server-version">Conn/3</a>
      <a href="#conn-enable-encryption">Conn/5</a><a href="#conn-logout">Conn/6</a><a href="#conn-command-10">Conn/10</a>
      <a href="#conn-regulator-update">Conn/11</a><a href="#conn-pong">Conn/12</a><a href="#conn-message-frame">Conn/13</a>
      <a href="#conn-game-site-info">Conn/14</a>
    </p>

    <Endpoint id="conn-boom" direction="server → client" phase="failure path" structure="logical order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/1</code> Connection::Boom</Fragment>
      <p>Terminates the native session with a numeric protocol error.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct Boom {
    error: u16, // Error::Code
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-server-version" direction="server → client" phase="protected bootstrap" structure="verified reflected order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/3</code> Connection::ServerVersion</Fragment>
      <p>The 32-bit payload uses the embedded <code>ServerVersion</code> type. This
      marked type is verified as reflection-compatible.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct ServerVersion {
    version: u32,
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-enable-encryption" direction="client → server" phase="last plaintext record" structure="empty payload" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/5</code> Connection::EnableEncryption</Fragment>
      <p>Starts independent inbound and outbound RC4 streams. Protection starts
      immediately after this empty record.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct EnableEncryption; // zero payload bits`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-logout" direction="client → server" phase="shutdown" structure="empty payload" status="capture verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/6</code> Connection::LogoutRequest</Fragment>
      <p>Ends the native session. The request has no payload fields.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct LogoutRequest; // zero payload bits`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-command-10" direction="client → server" phase="protected keepalive" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/10</code> Connection::Ping</Fragment>
      <p>The client sends this record approximately one time each minute. The
      optional signed value is a Unix epoch timestamp in microseconds. The server
      requires this keepalive and closes idle connections.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct Ping {
    time_data: Option<i64>, // Unix epoch microseconds
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-regulator-update" direction="server → client" phase="plaintext or protected" structure="logical order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/11</code> Connection::RegulatorUpdate</Fragment>
      <p>Updates the connection rate regulator. This record can occur before
      ResumeResponse.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct RegulatorUpdate {
    info: RegulatorInfo,
}

enum RegulatorInfo {
    None,
    LeakyBucket { threshold: u32, rate: u32 },
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-pong" direction="client → server" phase="protected keepalive" structure="verified reflected order" status="capture verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/12</code> Connection::Pong</Fragment>
      <p>Replies to connection timing traffic. It carries the same optional
      microsecond timestamp shape as Ping.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct Pong {
    time_data: Option<i64>, // Unix epoch microseconds
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-message-frame" direction="bidirectional" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/13</code> Connection::MessageFrame</Fragment>
      <p>Carries a generic application envelope. An inner route can select another
      service by FourCC and command, independently of the outer service slot.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct MessageFrame {
    payload: Vec<u8>,          // 0..=14,336 bytes
    frame_type: FrameType,     // u8 on the wire
    headers: Vec<FrameHeader>, // count: 0..=32
}

enum FrameHeader {
    Content(ContentHeader),
    Route(RouteHeader),
    Target(TargetHeader),
    Correlation(CorrelationHeader),
    Client(ClientHeader),
    Service(ServiceHeader),
    Error(ErrorHeader),
    Replicate(ReplicateHeader),
    Timestamp(TimestampHeader),
    Stream(StreamHeader),
    TraceRoute(TraceRouteHeader),
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">Reflection lists frame type and headers before the
      payload. Using that order corrupts the following record.</p>
      <p class="endpoint-note">Retail sends two maintenance requests to
      <code>TrnC</code> one time each minute. Command 3 contains four zero bytes.
      Command 2 contains <code>030080057e4002020a0301</code>. Both requests are
      <code>CS_ROUTED</code> frames. Their correlation headers set
      <code>reply = false</code>. A retail-compatible client must send these
      requests and <code>Conn/10 Ping</code>.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="conn-game-site-info" direction="server → client" phase="first protected bootstrap" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Conn/14</code> Connection::GameSiteInfo</Fragment>
      <p>Announces the client's public IPv4 endpoint as seen by the service, plus
      the available SC2 game sites.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct GameSiteInfo {
    external_ipv4_addr: Ipv4AddressPort,
    site_data: Vec<SiteDataForClient>, // 7-bit element count; metadata maximum 64
}

struct SiteDataForClient {
    name: Vec<u8>, // 6-bit byte count; metadata maximum 32
    address_port: Option<Ipv4AddressPort>,
}

struct Ipv4AddressPort {
    address: u32,
    port: u16,
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note"><b>Wire sequence:</b> external address and port,
      then a 7-bit site count, then that many <code>SiteDataForClient</code>
      values. The seven-bit count encodes the actual length; the embedded BSN
      metadata supplies the validation maximum of 64.</p>
    </Endpoint>
</ServiceSection>
